[
    {
        "content": "<p>Currently, <code>HasBasis</code> is defined as</p>\n<div class=\"codehilite\" data-code-language=\"Lean4\"><pre><span></span><code><span class=\"sd\">/-- We say that a filter `l` has a basis `s : ι → Set α` bounded by `p : ι → Prop`,</span>\n<span class=\"sd\">if `t ∈ l` if and only if `t` includes `s i` for some `i` such that `p i`. -/</span>\n<span class=\"kn\">structure</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">HasBasis</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">(</span><span class=\"n\">l</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">Filter</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">α</span><span class=\"o\">)</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">(</span><span class=\"n\">p</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">ι</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">→</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"kt\">Prop</span><span class=\"o\">)</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">(</span><span class=\"n\">s</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">ι</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">→</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">Set</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">α</span><span class=\"o\">)</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"kt\">Prop</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"kn\">where</span>\n<span class=\"w\">  </span><span class=\"n\">mem_iff'</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">∀</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">t</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">Set</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">α</span><span class=\"o\">,</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">t</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">∈</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">l</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">↔</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">∃</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">i</span><span class=\"o\">,</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">p</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">i</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">∧</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">s</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">i</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">⊆</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">t</span>\n</code></pre></div>\n<p>I notice that if <code>p</code> is omitted, or equivalently having <code>p</code> always return <code>True</code>, as follows:</p>\n<div class=\"codehilite\" data-code-language=\"Lean4\"><pre><span></span><code><span class=\"kn\">structure</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">HasBasis</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">{</span><span class=\"n\">α</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"kt\">Type</span><span class=\"bp\">*</span><span class=\"o\">}</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">{</span><span class=\"n\">ι</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"kt\">Sort</span><span class=\"bp\">*</span><span class=\"o\">}</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">(</span><span class=\"n\">l</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">Filter</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">α</span><span class=\"o\">)</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">(</span><span class=\"n\">s</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">ι</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">→</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">Set</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">α</span><span class=\"o\">)</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"kt\">Prop</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"kn\">where</span>\n<span class=\"w\">  </span><span class=\"n\">mem_iff'</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">∀</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">t</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">Set</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">α</span><span class=\"o\">,</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">t</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">∈</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">l</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">↔</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">∃</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">i</span><span class=\"o\">,</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">s</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">i</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">⊆</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">t</span>\n</code></pre></div>\n<p>then it seems to be almost equivalent, in the sense that</p>\n<div class=\"codehilite\" data-code-language=\"Lean4\"><pre><span></span><code><span class=\"kn\">example</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">{</span><span class=\"n\">α</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"kt\">Type</span><span class=\"bp\">*</span><span class=\"o\">}</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">{</span><span class=\"n\">ι</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"kt\">Sort</span><span class=\"bp\">*</span><span class=\"o\">}</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">{</span><span class=\"n\">l</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">Filter</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">α</span><span class=\"o\">}</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">{</span><span class=\"n\">p</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">ι</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">→</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"kt\">Prop</span><span class=\"o\">}</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">{</span><span class=\"n\">s</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">ι</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">→</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">Set</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">α</span><span class=\"o\">}:</span>\n<span class=\"w\">    </span><span class=\"n\">HasBasis</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">l</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">p</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">s</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">↔</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">HasBasis'</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">l</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">(</span><span class=\"k\">fun</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">i</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">{</span><span class=\"n\">i</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">ι</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">//</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">p</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">i</span><span class=\"o\">}</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">=&gt;</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">s</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">i</span><span class=\"o\">)</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:=</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"k\">by</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">_</span>\n\n<span class=\"kn\">example</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">{</span><span class=\"n\">α</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"kt\">Type</span><span class=\"bp\">*</span><span class=\"o\">}</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">{</span><span class=\"n\">ι</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"kt\">Sort</span><span class=\"bp\">*</span><span class=\"o\">}</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">{</span><span class=\"n\">l</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">Filter</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">α</span><span class=\"o\">}</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">{</span><span class=\"n\">s</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">ι</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">→</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">Set</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">α</span><span class=\"o\">}:</span>\n<span class=\"w\">    </span><span class=\"n\">HasBasis'</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">l</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">s</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">↔</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">HasBasis</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">l</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">(</span><span class=\"k\">fun</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">_</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">ι</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">=&gt;</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">True</span><span class=\"o\">)</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">s</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:=</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"k\">by</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">_</span>\n</code></pre></div>\n<p>So what's the reason for having the <code>p</code> there?</p>",
        "id": 630713469,
        "sender_full_name": "user202729",
        "timestamp": 1791697013
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>(By the way: what you have posted above is not a full proof of equivalence, and also not a <a href=\"https://leanprover-community.github.io/mwe.html\">#mwe</a>, which makes this harder to follow.)<br>\nWhat have you tried to answer this question yourself? For instance, was there any discussion about this when <code>HasBasis</code> was introduced? Are there any uses of this in mathlib that use the predicate?</p>",
        "id": 630737960,
        "sender_full_name": "Michael Rothgang",
        "timestamp": 1791716622
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>I suppose for equivalence one would also need to prove that the two transformations round-trips. They're not definitionally equal (because of the additional wrapping of <code>// True</code>), but they're obviously propositionally equal (proof irrelevance), which seems enough to me. It seems to me that putting the proof in compared to say <code>sorry</code> only give clutter.</p>\n<p>It has been like that since mathlib3, with no discussion of the other alternative. <a href=\"https://github.com/leanprover-community/mathlib3/pull/1896/changes#diff-ca356ee64faa68b6045a6f46e2c9edc9949a5a6653a1476adcc93c284bf22169R48-R51\">https://github.com/leanprover-community/mathlib3/pull/1896/changes#diff-ca356ee64faa68b6045a6f46e2c9edc9949a5a6653a1476adcc93c284bf22169R48-R51</a><br>\nOf course there are uses in mathlib that use the predicate, but as far as I can see, it can be equivalently written to use the other form.</p>",
        "id": 630752120,
        "sender_full_name": "user202729",
        "timestamp": 1791722477
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>The current definition is simply much more convenient, although this fact is a little counter-intuitive.</p>",
        "id": 630767376,
        "sender_full_name": "Patrick Massot",
        "timestamp": 1791731836
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>Of course they both cover the same mathematical content, and they are both a bit annoying in certain cases. The point is that handling a useless <code>_ ↦ True</code> is less annoying than handling a subtype.</p>",
        "id": 630767481,
        "sender_full_name": "Patrick Massot",
        "timestamp": 1791731914
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>It's not so counterintuitive to understand why it's more convenient, if you take the right perspective. </p>\n<p>In particular, consider that:</p>\n<ol>\n<li>It is frequently the case that you want to index on only some elements of a type (e.g., only the positive real numbers, or only the open sets containing a fixed point), so much so that having a nontrivial predicate is the norm rather than the exception. So occurrences where we use <code>True</code> is comparatively uncommon.</li>\n<li>Combining subtypes is much more of a hassle than combining predicates. If you want to restrict a basis to a smaller collection, then that would be taking a subtype of a subtype (gross), but for predicates, it's just <code>And</code>.</li>\n</ol>",
        "id": 630793979,
        "sender_full_name": "Jireh Loreaux",
        "timestamp": 1791746824
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>Follow up question: why is this defined using a map into sets rather than a set of sets?</p>",
        "id": 630821796,
        "sender_full_name": "Artie Khovanov",
        "timestamp": 1791763015
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>Essentially the same reason: convenience. Often times it's nicer to work with an indexed family, and if you want the set of sets, you can always recover it via <a href=\"https://leanprover-community.github.io/mathlib4_docs/find/?pattern=Filter.HasBasis.isBasis#doc\">docs#Filter.HasBasis.isBasis</a>. But you can't really recover a particularly nice index type from just a bare set of sets.</p>",
        "id": 630861816,
        "sender_full_name": "Jireh Loreaux",
        "timestamp": 1791779867
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>Oh I see<br>\nI wonder why we don't do this for finite sums<br>\nI guess it's just what index types come up in practice?</p>",
        "id": 630952174,
        "sender_full_name": "Artie Khovanov",
        "timestamp": 1791802054
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>Huh, I thought the follow-up question is an exercise for me or something. <span aria-label=\"sweat smile\" class=\"emoji emoji-1f605\" role=\"img\" title=\"sweat smile\">:sweat_smile:</span> </p>\n<p>Anyway, apparently there's this comment at the top of the file… (feel pretty silly to not notice this)<br>\nI propose to move it right before the definition of <code>HasBasis</code>, since after all, the implementation note <em>is</em> specifically about <code>HasBasis</code>.</p>\n<div class=\"codehilite\" data-code-language=\"Lean4\"><pre><span></span><code><span class=\"bp\">##</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">Implementation</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">notes</span>\n\n<span class=\"n\">As</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"k\">with</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"ss\">`Set.iUnion</span><span class=\"bp\">`/</span><span class=\"ss\">`biUnion</span><span class=\"bp\">`/</span><span class=\"ss\">`Set.sUnion</span><span class=\"bp\">`</span><span class=\"o\">,</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">there</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">are</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">three</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">different</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">approaches</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">to</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">filter</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">bases</span><span class=\"o\">:</span>\n\n<span class=\"bp\">*</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"ss\">`Filter.HasBasis</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">l</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">s</span><span class=\"bp\">`</span><span class=\"o\">,</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"ss\">`s</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">Set</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">(</span><span class=\"n\">Set</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">α</span><span class=\"o\">)</span><span class=\"bp\">`;</span>\n<span class=\"bp\">*</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"ss\">`Filter.HasBasis</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">l</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">s</span><span class=\"bp\">`</span><span class=\"o\">,</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"ss\">`s</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">ι</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">→</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">Set</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">α</span><span class=\"bp\">`;</span>\n<span class=\"bp\">*</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"ss\">`Filter.HasBasis</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">l</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">p</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">s</span><span class=\"bp\">`</span><span class=\"o\">,</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"ss\">`p</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">ι</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">→</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"kt\">Prop</span><span class=\"bp\">`</span><span class=\"o\">,</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"ss\">`s</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">ι</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">→</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">Set</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">α</span><span class=\"bp\">`.</span>\n\n<span class=\"n\">We</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">use</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">the</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">latter</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">one</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">because</span><span class=\"o\">,</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">e</span><span class=\"bp\">.</span><span class=\"n\">g</span><span class=\"bp\">.</span><span class=\"o\">,</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"ss\">`𝓝</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">x</span><span class=\"bp\">`</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"k\">in</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">an</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"ss\">`EMetricSpace</span><span class=\"bp\">`</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">or</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"k\">in</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">a</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"ss\">`MetricSpace</span><span class=\"bp\">`</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">has</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">a</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">basis</span>\n<span class=\"n\">of</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">this</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">form</span><span class=\"bp\">.</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">The</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">other</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">two</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">can</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">be</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">emulated</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"kn\">using</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"ss\">`s</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">=</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">id</span><span class=\"bp\">`</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">or</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"ss\">`p</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">=</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"k\">fun</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">_</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">↦</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">True</span><span class=\"bp\">`.</span>\n\n<span class=\"n\">With</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">this</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">approach</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">sometimes</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">one</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">needs</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">to</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"ss\">`simp</span><span class=\"bp\">`</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">the</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">statement</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">provided</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"k\">by</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">the</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"ss\">`Filter.HasBasis</span><span class=\"bp\">`</span>\n<span class=\"n\">machinery</span><span class=\"o\">,</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">e</span><span class=\"bp\">.</span><span class=\"n\">g</span><span class=\"bp\">.</span><span class=\"o\">,</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"ss\">`simp</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">only</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">[</span><span class=\"n\">true_and_iff</span><span class=\"o\">]</span><span class=\"bp\">`</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">or</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"ss\">`simp</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">only</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">[</span><span class=\"n\">forall_const</span><span class=\"o\">]</span><span class=\"bp\">`</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">can</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">help</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"k\">with</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">the</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">case</span>\n<span class=\"ss\">`p</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">=</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"k\">fun</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">_</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">↦</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">True</span><span class=\"bp\">`.</span>\n</code></pre></div>",
        "id": 630996141,
        "sender_full_name": "user202729",
        "timestamp": 1791810939
    },
    {
        "content": "<p><span class=\"user-mention silent\" data-user-id=\"400289\">Artie Khovanov</span> <a href=\"#narrow/channel/287929-mathlib4/topic/Purpose.20of.20.60HasBasis.60.20taking.20the.20.60.28p.20.3A.20.CE.B9.20.E2.86.92.20Prop.29.60.20argument.3F/near/630952174\">said</a>:</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p>I wonder why we don't do this for finite sums</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>I didn't understand this comment</p>",
        "id": 630997455,
        "sender_full_name": "Jireh Loreaux",
        "timestamp": 1791811196
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>I think there's a lot of value in having implementation notes at the top of the file, in the module doc-string. I wouldn't want to <em>move</em> this comment. However, <em>adding</em> a comment to <code>HasBasis</code> (this can be a short reference to the implementation note) could be useful.</p>",
        "id": 630998467,
        "sender_full_name": "Michael Rothgang",
        "timestamp": 1791811359
    },
    {
        "content": "<p><span class=\"user-mention\" data-user-id=\"197836\">@Jireh Loreaux</span> finite sums are defined over finite sets, and over functions from finite types, but not over functions from finite types bounded by a predicate</p>",
        "id": 631000923,
        "sender_full_name": "Artie Khovanov",
        "timestamp": 1791811702
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>Ah, because for that we use <a href=\"https://leanprover-community.github.io/mathlib4_docs/find/?pattern=Finset.filter#doc\">docs#Finset.filter</a> and that <em>does</em> take a predicate.</p>",
        "id": 631001425,
        "sender_full_name": "Jireh Loreaux",
        "timestamp": 1791811773
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>This is codified in the <code>∑ a ∈ s with p a, f a</code> notation.</p>",
        "id": 631001786,
        "sender_full_name": "Jireh Loreaux",
        "timestamp": 1791811824
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>(and in the <code>Fintype</code> case, note that <code>∑ i, f i</code> just means <code>∑ i ∈ Finset.univ, f i</code>.)</p>",
        "id": 631002106,
        "sender_full_name": "Jireh Loreaux",
        "timestamp": 1791811872
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>Doesn't that lead to the same problem you described when applying successive predicates?<br>\nAlthough <a href=\"https://leanprover-community.github.io/mathlib4_docs/find/?pattern=Finset.filter_filter#doc\">docs#Finset.filter_filter</a> looks a bit nicer than <a href=\"https://leanprover-community.github.io/mathlib4_docs/find/?pattern=Equiv.subtypeSubtypeEquivSubtypeInter#doc\">docs#Equiv.subtypeSubtypeEquivSubtypeInter</a>, so I guess the key here is that <code>Finset.filter</code> stays in the same type (and also it isn't a type so equality makes sense).</p>",
        "id": 631005316,
        "sender_full_name": "Snir Broshi",
        "timestamp": 1791812344
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>Also, the predicate for <code>HasBasis</code> could have been based into the filter, not the subtype. So given two choices between in-filter and in-type, cut the gordian knot and keep it unbundled.</p>",
        "id": 631041722,
        "sender_full_name": "Yakov Pechersky",
        "timestamp": 1791819738
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>Ok, but now you have three choices!</p>",
        "id": 631050160,
        "sender_full_name": "Eric Wieser",
        "timestamp": 1791821593
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>Note that we now live in a time where this question is easier than ever to answer experimentally; you can ask an AI system to do the refactor of removing the argument, and then look at the result to get a feel for what the fallout is. I'm not necessarily saying that the resulting refactor will be mathlib-ready, but it is certainly a good experiment.</p>",
        "id": 631050504,
        "sender_full_name": "Eric Wieser",
        "timestamp": 1791821686
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>The subtype-as-clunky argument I think is the best, since propeq predicates make nondefeq subtypes, which then require data to convert between them.</p>",
        "id": 631072890,
        "sender_full_name": "Yakov Pechersky",
        "timestamp": 1791827435
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>Speaking of which, which form of the following is recommended, <code>⟨h⟩</code> versus <code>hasBasis_iff.mpr h</code> versus <code>hasBasis_iff.2 h</code> versus <code>where mem_iff' := h</code>?</p>\n<p>(The last form is only applicable in limited cases, such as at top-level.)</p>\n<p>Reason: if one change the implementation of <code>HasBasis</code> to be no longer an inductive type containing a single field of the type it currently is, the versions that use <code>hasBasis_iff</code> will stay, but other versions won't. On the other hand, the other versions are more verbose.</p>\n<p>In a proof, there is also option of <code>:= ⟨by …⟩</code> versus <code>:= by rw [hasBasis_iff]; …</code>.</p>\n<p>(Context: <code>HasBasis</code> is an inductive prop that contain a single argument, and <code>hasBasis_iff</code> says <code>l.HasBasis p s ↔ ∀ t, t ∈ l ↔ ∃ i, p i ∧ s i ⊆ t</code>, so <code>hasBasis_iff.mpr</code> has the same type as the constructor.)</p>",
        "id": 631648108,
        "sender_full_name": "user202729",
        "timestamp": 1791984153
    }
]